PROBLEM 1 : 
816
Solution  : int maxProfitAssignment(vector<int>& difficulty, vector<int>& profit, vector<int>& worker) {
        vector<pair<int,int>>jobs;
        int n = difficulty.size();
        for(int i = 0 ;i < n;i++){
            jobs.push_back({difficulty[i],profit[i]});
        }
        sort(jobs.begin(),jobs.end());
        sort(worker.begin(),worker.end());
        int maxProfit = 0;
        int ans = 0;
        int i =0;
        for(int j = 0; j < worker.size(); j++){
            while( i < profit.size() && worker[j] >= jobs[i].first){
                maxProfit = max(maxProfit, jobs[i].second);
                i++;
            }
            ans += maxProfit;
        }
        return ans;

    }

A stronger worker can perform every job that a weaker worker can.
Once the jobs are sorted by difficulty, we never need to revisit previously processed jobs.
The answer for a worker is not the profit of the current job, but the maximum profit among all jobs they can perform.



Question: Why is it safe to never move the job pointer backward?
Answer: Because workers are processed in increasing order of skill. Every new worker can perform all jobs that previous workers could perform,
so those jobs never need to be reconsidered. Only new jobs need to be checked, while bestProfit remembers the best profit seen so far.
